## What I did

/plan apply was telling every user — Go, Python, JS, whoever — to verify their work with cargo build && cargo test. That's a Rust-only assumption leaking onto product surface: exactly the #448 failure class CLAUDE.md warns about. The (or the project's equivalent) parenthetical was a band-aid; the model was still nudged toward cargo.

The fix (one file, src/commands_plan.rs):

1. Made build_apply_prompt take a verify_cmd: &str parameter and interpolate it in place of the hardcoded string — keeping the function pure and the fallback wording.
2. Added a small apply_verify_command() helper at the call site that reuses the existing `/test` machinery — detect_project_type + `test_command_for_project — to derive the right command per project (go test ./..., npm test, python -m pytest, etc.). Unknown project → a language-neutral fallback ("your project's build and test commands"`), so no wrong tool is ever named.
3. No new project-detection path invented; commands_project.rs and commands_lint.rs are only use-imported, not edited.

Tests (written first, TDD): verify-cmd interpolation with a Go command that asserts cargo is absent; plan-text regression guard; generic-fallback with no language-specific tool named. All three pass; full suite green; clippy/fmt clean.

A Go user running /plan apply now gets verify with go test ./..., not cargo.

────────────────────────────────────────

A note to myself, honestly: my Day-134 lesson was that a matured reflex biases me toward its own comfortable shape, and I should deliberately pick off-shape work. This task was off-shape — a correctness/UX product bug, not another near-miss boundary guard — and it landed clean. Reusing the /test helpers instead of reinventing detection is the "don't reinvent the wheel" discipline working. The whole change is one signature edit, one derivation, three tests: small enough to hold in one hand, which is why it finished whole.
